{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review round six at exact clean pushed head 5bc84ca2f3dbfd57258450ce76f32ed7c37b6922 on request/repo-remote-link. Checkout clean, origin matching, base c679fb73.\n\nThis answers your ratified verdict 342e9cfc. All three blockers were about making claims true rather than making the fix stronger, and two of the three were sentences I published.\n\nPATH IS GONE and the inherited set is empty: command.Env is a clone of five stated entries and nothing else. The allowlist, the case-folding comparison, the admission predicate and the duplicate-name edge are deleted with it. You were right that my own measurement already implied this and I had not acted on it.\n\nONE SURVIVOR, named rather than smuggled: slices.Clone. It is not an admission rule — it stops a caller who edits one command's Env from rewriting what the next runs under — and it has its own test that fails when it becomes the shared package slice.\n\nTHE TRUST SCOPE IS NOW MEASURED. Repository-local configuration, worktree configuration at GIT_DIR/config.worktree where extensions.worktreeConfig is set, and include.path and includeIf targets absolute or relative, with core.fsmonitor observed executing from all five placements during an ordinary status read under the stated environment. Not \"the repository's own configuration file\", which was the one case I had exercised and published as the boundary.\n\n--no-includes IS SHIPPED AND CANNOT BE MUTATION-TESTED, which I want ruled on rather than accepted. I verified the behaviour myself: --local already declines includes, --local --includes brings the value back, --no-includes matches the default. So the flag pins a documented Git default and no mutation can distinguish it while that default holds. I judged that stating this plainly beats either omitting the flag or claiming a sensitivity it cannot have — but a flag that guards nothing is also a thing a reviewer may reasonably want gone, and you should say which.\n\nA CONSEQUENCE I FOUND WHILE CHECKING THAT, and it is user-facing rather than security. A remote configured through an include is not read at all, so the browser shows no link for it. Limitation, not defect, and it is in the internal/app and ui artifacts because someone will hit it and look for a bug in the link logic.\n\nA COVERAGE HOLE CLOSED, and this is the finding I would most like a second opinion on. Deleting --local from the remote read passed the entire suite before this head. Closing the environment had made an older guard untestable: with no outer scope reachable, nothing exercised the flag that bounds which scope is read. There is now a test that fails when --local goes, with hard controls. I think the general shape — a security fix silently disarming a neighbouring test — is worth more than this instance of it.\n\nTHE PLATFORM CONTRACT IS NARROWED, not asserted. Closed environment stated for Unix where it is exercised; Windows named as outside the contract, because the package builds for it, nothing here has run on it, and Go's os/exec appends SYSTEMROOT from the parent when the child carries no name folding to it. I verified addCriticalEnv in the Go source. No Windows regression was written, deliberately: a test that has never executed is not evidence and would give the claim a false witness.\n\nTHE CASE-VARIED TEST WAS REBUILT, not deleted. With nothing admitted there is no case to compare, so it now asserts the stronger claim the empty set allows, one layer further out: every vector including case-varied spellings set into the real process environment, driving the constructor all five reads go through, requiring exactly the five stated entries and nothing else.\n\nMUTATIONS. Appending os.Environ() fires all six vector regressions and both closed-world tests; dropping any stated pin fails its own vector; sharing the slice fails the isolation test; dropping --local now fails where it did not before; the citation exit-code rule still fails when made fail-open; dropping --no-includes fails nothing, stated rather than hidden.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 34 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached — cmd/gs 241.071s, internal/app 167.353s, internal/kernel 162.402s, internal/docset 106.451s. npm test 131 pass 0 fail. gofmt empty; build, vet, GOOS=windows build of internal/app, diff-check all exit 0; make ui leaves git status empty; make ui-check exit 0. Not run by me: go test -race, and any gate on Linux.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"branch":"request/repo-remote-link","conditions":"Independent review of exact clean pushed head 5bc84ca2f3dbfd57258450ce76f32ed7c37b6922 on request/repo-remote-link, citing all seven live artifacts at that head: 1e9aa3d3 internal/app, b192f34e internal/service, 479c7361 ui, 56287b99 internal/service/uidist, d23b538d docs/reference/architecture.md, 1f0c8f8f docs/reference/gs/serve.md, 9fa5bb9f docs/concepts/components.md. Governing request is planner's f42f5e35; my promise is 8bdb5b67. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions and file the verdict with gs review resting on all seven artifacts. SECURITY: judge whether the empty inherited set is genuinely empty on the path all five commands take; whether the named trust scope is now complete; whether every claim in code, artifacts and the architecture page names only what was exercised and on which platform; and whether shipping a flag that no mutation can distinguish is the right call or should be dropped. Do not merge.","head":"5bc84ca2f3dbfd57258450ce76f32ed7c37b6922","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}